a level 3 program has to end below 0ff0, because micronix loads at
0x1000: everything it has - text, data and bss together - and the
kernel's object header is read in at 0ff0.  that is not a preference.
a byte past it is overwritten by the first block of the load, and it
presents as a boot that reads perfectly and then enters nothing.
mkbootimg measures every level 3 against it and refuses one that reaches
it, so an image whose loader has outgrown its 4k is a failed build and
not a silent one.  boot.c says the same thing at more length.

in a pinch, the boot code could put all buffers except the indirect
block into a non-loaded BSS above 4k; that will gain 512 bytes.

this directory contains the standalone and cp/m boot programs
for the djdma, hddma, ide, ncr 5380 and hdca controllers.

a boot happens in four levels, numbered by where the code comes from and
not by who reads it.  level 0 is the rom on the mpz80 board, whichever
one is in the machine - mon 5.0, mon 4.47, anything else.  level 1 is the
four kilobyte rom in the socket on a memory board, a multio or an ss1,
stand/roms/l1boot.s, which the monitor copies down to f000 and enters,
and which reads block 0 off whichever controller answers.  level 2 is
that block: sector 0 of the boot disk, entered at 0100.  level 3 is the
four kilobyte program that knows about the micronix filesystem, which
level 2 reads to 8000 and unpacks at 0100.

the two roms are levels 0 and 1 and the two things this directory builds
are levels 2 and 3, whichever controller they are in front of.  the
floppy is not an exception to any of that any more: the dj-dma's own
bootstrap - the one that halted the cpu, planted a routine at 0038 and
copied 128 bytes of track 0 to 0080 - is switched off at the card (J2 A
to B), so its block 0 is a boot block like every other medium's, and the
level 1 rom drives its channel program itself.

level 2 is the sector the rom reads to 0100.  it moves itself out of the
way, reads level 3 to 8000, unpacks the object header and jumps to its
text.  level 3 is one source - boot.c - and five drivers, because what
differs is how a block is read: mwio.c for the HD-DMA, djio.c for the
floppy, ideio.c for the IDE card, ncrio.c for the NCR 5380 and hdcaio.c
for the HDCA.  so there are five level 3 programs, and each belongs to
the level 2 sector that loads it:

	mwboot.com	the hard disk		loaded by mwboot1.s
	djboot.com	the floppy		loaded by djboot1.s
	ideboot.com	the ide card		loaded by ideboot1.s
	ncrboot.com	the ncr 5380		loaded by ncrboot1.s
	hdcaboot.com	the hdca		loaded by hdcaboot1.s

all five are linked at 0100, which is both where cp/m loads a .com and
where the monitor's hard disk boot enters, so one binary serves either
way in.

mwboot1.s is the level 2 sector for the monitor's way in.  the rom reads
one sector, 512 bytes, to 0100 and jumps to it; mwboot.com is several
sectors, so something has to fetch the rest, and that sector is this.
it moves itself to c000 first, because what it is loading is linked at
0100 and would otherwise land on the loop doing the loading.

it sets up almost nothing.  the rom used the channel at 0080 to read this
block in, so the constants are loaded, the drive is selected and the
heads are at cylinder 0 - only the sector, the dma address and the
opcode change.  65 bytes of the 512.

the other four sectors are the same shape for their own cards, and each
is written around what its card does not have.  ideboot1.s has no
command block in memory to be handed a sector with, so it writes the
drive's task file and moves the sector through the 8255 by hand.
ncrboot1.s drives the 5380's req/ack handshake a byte at a time - select
the target, send a READ(10), take the data, take the status and the
message.  hdcaboot1.s writes the hdca's own header bytes - head, track,
sector, key - through the data port and pulls the sector out a byte at a
time.  all four fit in the half of the sector ahead of the label, and
mkbootimg refuses one that has grown into it.

the floppy image is laid out around the same label, and block 0 is where
level 2 and the label both have to be:

	block 0		level 2, in the front half, and the label in the
			half behind it
	the rest of	zeros.  on an eight inch diskette this is not a
	cylinder 0	whole number of blocks: cylinder 0 head 0 there is
			single density, 26 sectors of 128 bytes, and block
			0 is its first four
	cylinder 1	level 3, djboot.com, from its first sector

block 0 is four sectors on an eight inch drive and one on a five inch
one, which is the rom's business and not the loader's: it reads block 0
off whichever drive answers - four 128 byte reads there, one 512 byte
read here - and hands over the 512 bytes it made.  level 3 begins a
cylinder up because the rest of an eight inch cylinder 0 is 128 byte
sectors, which is no place for four kilobytes, and every medium's boot
area owns cylinders 0 and 1 anyway: the label's toff is 2 on all of
them.  so mkbootimg lays an image out as block 0, filled to the end of
cylinder 0, and then level 3 - which is therefore at one address in the
image for every medium.

mkfs -i puts mwboot1/mwboot in a file that owns cylinder 0, block 0
being level 2 and block 1 onward level 3.  the second half of block 0
holds the disk label (sys/dlabel.h), which is why level 2 has to fit in
the first half.  a diskette's image carries its label itself; a hard
disk's does not, because the label is the drive's and mkfs -i takes the
geometry from the device node and writes the label into the block it
puts the boot in.


tasks and maps
--------------

everything above says what each level does.  where it does it is the
MPZ80's business: a boot starts in one task, runs its middle in another,
and hands the kernel back to the first.

the cpu has sixteen tasks, each with its own 64k cut into sixteen 4k
pages.  a task's map is 32 bytes - one page number and one access byte
per 4k - and it lives in a ram at 600h that can be written but not read
back: task 0's at 600h, task 1's at 620h, 20h apiece.  task 0 is the
only supervisor.

what makes a boot interesting is that the supervisor's low page is not
memory.  in task 0 an address below 1000h does not reach the s-100 bus
at all: it is the cpu board's own page - its ram, the prom, the map
itself, and the registers the map is written through.  in every other
task those addresses are the first 4k of bus memory instead.  so the two
kinds of code in a boot run in different tasks:

	task 0	the monitor, and the level 1 rom it copies down and
		enters - stand/roms/l1boot.s, at f000
	task 1	the level 2 sector, the level 3 loader, and the
		kernel's own entry

the kernel decides it.  it is linked at 1000 and wants everything above
it, and it cannot start any lower, because below 1000 the supervisor has
no memory to give it.  the levels that have to live under 1000 - the
sector, the loader, their buffers - can only live in a user task, since
that is the only place under 1000 that is memory at all.  the trap back
runs the other way: only the supervisor can take it, because the trap
entry is in the prom, in the board's page, which no user task can
address.

the supervisor's page
---------------------

in task 0, below 1000h.  this is the whole of the interface between the
prom, the monitor and the kernel, and the kernel's own symbols for it
are in sys/uhdr.s.

	0000	super	a jump to 1000h, written at reset: where the
			trap handler calls into the kernel
	0003	user	a jump to the user's pc, rebuilt in front of
			every return to user mode
	0006	ctask	the task a trap came from, and
	0007	cmask	the mask to go back with.  the trap saves them
			and the return restores them as one word
	0008	cstack	where the trap handler builds its frame
	000a	sp 000c pc 000e de 0010 hl 0012 af
			what the prom's trap entry pushes first
	0014	begsav	the trap entry's own frame, 30 bytes to 0031
	0032	bounce	256 bytes: staging for reading a rom that lives
			above the first megabyte, where a bank switch
			hides all of the machine's own low memory
	0132	conram	the console driver the monitor builds at reset
	0166	ramhome	the level 0 copy loop, the thing that reads the
			level 1 rom out of its socket
	01b0	gobuff	the task switch stub, rewritten by every switch
	01e0	oldstk	the bottom of the trap frame, 32 bytes to 01ff
	0200	the monitor's scratch: its image of the map, which
			cannot be read back, and the stack the prom's
			trap frame is built down from
	0400	locio	400 trap address (r) / display segment (w),
			401 keyboard (r) / display column (w),
			402 switch port (r) / task register (w),
			403 trap status (r) / mask register (w)
	0600	mapram	the map itself, write only
	0800	eprom	the half of the prom any trap enables; its
			last sixteen bytes, 0bf0 to 0bff, are the
			trap entry

the prom below 800h is visible to the supervisor only during a reset.
that is the reason the monitor keeps two resident copies: it moves its
own copy loop to ramhome, and then that loop copies the level 1 rom out
of its socket and into ram at f000.  neither can be run where it was
assembled.

what the map holds
------------------

every change a boot makes to a map, in order:

	at reset	task 0	segments 0-15 -> pages 0-15
			task 1	segments 0-15 -> pages 0-15, so in
				task 1 virtual is physical
			the reset entry also points task 0's segment 1
			at page 0 - its comment says "t0 map points to
			t1 map seg 0" - so that the supervisor can see
			what task 1 sees at 0000
	the monitor	setup points segment 1 at page 0 again: "a
			window for DMA device commands"
	the copy loop	it points segment 1 at page 0f0h, the rom's own
			socket above the first megabyte, and segment 2 at
			page 0fh, where the copy lands.  the rom is entered
			at f000 with both still set
	in the rom	a row that hands a controller an address points
			segment 1 at page 0 itself, so that the block it
			is handing over has an address
	the switch	the rom points segment 1 at page 1 and segment 2
			at page 2 - task 0 is the identity again - and
			enters the block as task 1

the two maps agreeing at every address is the whole of the arrangement:
the kernel is linked for the identity, and the trap the kernel takes
executes in task 0 at the same addresses that it was loaded at in task
1, because the prom builds the trap frame and jumps into the kernel
without either map changing.

the window onto bus page 0
--------------------------

this is what the supervisor's low page costs.  three of the controllers
are handed their work through memory in bus page 0 - the hd-dma is
pointed at its command block by three bytes at 0050h, the floppy runs a
channel program at 0050h, disk 3 keeps an iopb at 0010h - and in task 0
there is no address for that page: 0000 through 0fff is the board's own.

so the rom gives itself a second name for it.  pointing task 0's segment
1 at page 0 makes 1000h-1fffh bus page 0, and 1100h is bus 0100h.  every
row that needs it writes (mapram + 2) = 0 for itself, because the copy
loop left something else there and the next row may not assume it.

the transfer goes to bus 0200h, which the same window shows at 1200h,
and the row then copies the sector to (dest) through the window - into
the rom's own scratch while it is reading a label, or to 1100h for the
block the next level is entered at.  that last copy puts the block at
bus 0100h, which is where task 1 will look for it: the window is not a
copy, it is the same bytes under another name.

only the supervisor needs the trick.  task 1 is the identity, so its
0050h, 0080h and 0200h are bus memory, which is why the level 2 sector
and the level 3 loader name bus addresses directly.  the monitor's own
disk boot splits the same way: it builds its command block through the
window at 1050h and 1080h, and tells the controller to dma to 0100h,
because the block it is starting will run in task 1.

the switch
----------

the rom and the monitor switch tasks the same way, by writing a stub at
gobuff and jumping into it:

	ld a,<task>
	ld (402h),a
	nop			six of them
	jp <entry>

the stub lives in on-board ram and not in the prom because writing the
task register drops the prom's lower half - the code after the write is
not there any more.  the six nops are the hardware's seven-instruction
swap countdown, so that the jump is not fetched until the new task's map
is up.  the write covers only the task register, which leaves the mask
alone: the monitor set it to trap on halts and stops at reset, and that
mask is what makes a halt in a user task a system call.

the caller sets ctask to the same task number first.  a trap does not
carry the task it came from, so the trap handler reads ctask to tell the
supervisor's own traps - ctask zero - from a user task's.

and the entry is not the address the block was read to.  the block is
sitting in the window, whose base is 1000h, so the bus address - the
address task 1 sees - is dest minus 1000h: the rom takes the low three
nibbles of the high byte.  the hdca is the one medium that says
something here: its rom reads the two bytes at 1feh as the address it
enters, which is what mkbootimg's -a writes.

where the bytes are
-------------------

everything the levels after the rom touch is inside task 1's one 64k.

	0050h	three bytes pointing the hd-dma at its command block
	0080h	the command block - and, on the floppy, its channel
		program
	0100h	level 2, which moves itself up to c000h
	0200h	where a controller's dma lands before it is copied
	0ff0h	the kernel image's object header; the image is one flat
		read of 117 blocks through 0f9f0h
	1000h	level 3's buffers, and then the kernel's text
	8000h	level 3's staging for the object image
	c000h	level 2, out of the way
	10000h	the loader's stack, growing down - mwboot1 sets sp to 0,
		meaning 10000h, because everything below it is about to
		be the kernel

nothing in the kernel's image is relocated: the file's text offset is
the address it runs at, and the sections the linker put at c000h, e000h
and f000h are written to exactly those addresses.  that is only true in
task 1, where the map is the identity, and it is why the loader has to
be there.  the image begins at 0ff0h, sixteen bytes below where the
kernel's text starts, and those sixteen bytes are its object header -
which is lucky, because the kernel's entry sets its stack to 1000h and
grows down into them, and once it is in task 0 it cannot see below 1000h
at all.

the last of those sections, f000h, is the page the level 1 rom was
copied to: the kernel's u page is linked there, so the rom's own 4k
becomes the u page as the load runs past it.  the rom is finished by
then - the machine has been in task 1 since the block was entered - and
nothing reads it again.

the trap back
-------------

the loader does not trap.  it jumps: _enterk puts the inode it booted
from in HL and jumps to the loaded text at 1000h, in task 1 like
everything else in this directory.

1000h is not a routine but a jump with a live operand.  uhdr.s puts the
jump opcode there and, at 1001h, _trapvec, which the kernel reassigns.
what the load leaves there first sends it to boot, which sets sp to
1000h - growing down into the header the image was loaded from - keeps
HL in _kino, and jumps to _start.

_start is start() in sys/trap.c, and the trap is there:

	start() runs in task 1 and says so in its comment: "we are in
	task 1, and the memory maps for tasks 1 and 0 are identical".
	it points _trapvec at task0 and halts.

	a halt in a user task is a trap.  the cpu clears the task
	register before it fetches again, so it is task 0 and the
	supervisor.

	the prom's trap entry at 0bf0h takes over.  it saves the user's
	sp at 000ah, sets sp to begsav, pushes af, hl and de, and calls
	trappd.

	trappd gets the user's pc back by subtracting 15 - the length
	of the prom's own trap entry - from the return address of its
	call, then builds the rest of the frame: the alternate bank, ix,
	iy and the interrupt register, the primary bank, and then three
	words the kernel reads as a structure - the task and mask the
	trap came from, the user pc, and the user sp.  that is the 32
	bytes at 1e0h, which is the shape of the first 32 bytes of
	struct user (include/sys/proc.h).

	a trap with ctask nonzero and not from the stop switch calls
	super: 0000h, the jump to 1000h, which is now a jump through
	_trapvec - and _trapvec is &task0.

so the first thing the kernel does in task 0 is task0() in sys/uhdr.s,
which parks the firmware's return address and calls task0_body().  that
is the kernel proper.  it takes the kernel's mask, clears ustack, copies
the 32-byte frame out of 1e0h into u.stack, moves its stack into the u
page, points the trap stack at &u.save so that later traps build their
frames there rather than in the monitor's ram, and calls main().

by the time task0_body returns, the kernel has been copied into a bank of
its own and task 1's map points at that bank, so the bytes the loader
wrote at 1000h are no longer the ones that run.  task0_body forks process
1 - the code that was the loader's task - and returns through the
firmware: the trap return restores its registers, makes the jump at
0003h, and writes the task and mask word with one store, low byte to the
task register.  the task it returns to is task 1, at the instruction
after the halt in start(), which puts a halt at location 8 of that
process's own page so that rst 8 is a system call, and calls xinit().

what the levels below contributed, in order: the monitor built task 1's
identity map at reset and copied the level 1 rom into the page the
kernel's u page would be linked at; the level 1 rom pointed task 0's
segment 1 back at page 1 so that both maps would name the same bytes,
set ctask to 1 so that the trap would be read as a user task's, and
entered the block at the address the medium itself gave; the level 2
sector and the level 3 loader ran in task 1 and touched no map at all;
and the kernel's own entry took the trap that made it the supervisor.
